meta-rzg3l-fast-logo-intro
==========================

Boot logo for the RZ/G3L SMARC EVK, from a few hundred milliseconds after
reset until the first program in Linux draws on the display, with no black
screen and no HDMI re-sync in between.

U-Boot brings the MIPI DSI -> ADV7535 -> HDMI pipeline up, plays a short
sequence of frames once and stays on the last one. The kernel inherits that
running pipeline instead of re-creating it, so the last frame stays on screen
through the whole kernel boot. Whatever commits to the display first in Linux -
the compositor, or an application of your own - replaces it.

This layer contains no Linux application. A reference application that
continues the animation in Linux is delivered separately
(rzg3l-fast-logo-sample-app), and nothing in this layer depends on it.

Tested against Yocto scarthgap, RZ/G3L BSP v1.0.0, u-boot 2024.07,
linux-renesas 6.12.46-cip8, MACHINE = "smarc-rzg3l", DISTRO = "rz-vlp",
core-image-weston.


I. What happens at boot
=======================

  reset      BL2, BL31, U-Boot.
  U-Boot     rzg2l_video_init() runs from init_sequence_r, one entry before
             the main loop. It programs CPG, the DSI D-PHY and link, the
             ADV7535 over I2C, the DU and the VSPD, loads frame 00 from /boot
             and starts the display on it.
             *** frame 00 visible ***
             The remaining frames are loaded behind it and handed to the VSPD
             as a display list chain: each frame is held for logo_rate_ms,
             frame 00 included, and the last one repeats forever. The VSPD
             walks the chain by itself - U-Boot carries on booting.
  booti      A device tree fix-up tells the kernel it has a running pipeline
             to inherit, and which CEA mode it is running (section V).
  kernel     The frame buffers are reserved, the display clocks are critical,
             and the VSPD, DU, DSI and ADV7535 drivers skip their destructive
             setup for one modeset.
             *** last frame still visible ***
  userspace  The first DRM commit takes the plane over with a content change,
             not a mode change.

Nothing is waited for: the intro plays while U-Boot and the kernel carry on,
so the layer adds only the time to load the frames (about 70 ms each at
1080p, after the first one is already on screen).


II. Relationship with other layers and applications
===================================================

  meta-rzg3l-fast-logo, meta-rzg3l-fast-anime-logo
      MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE, and the build says so. All three carry the same
      boot logo series for u-boot and linux-renesas.

  meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base
      Independent. Both append u-boot and linux-renesas; the combination is
      expected to work but is not the tested configuration.

  Applications
      Nothing is installed or started in Linux by this layer, and weston-init
      is not touched. Any DRM/KMS client can take the display over; the
      sample application is one. See section VII for what an application
      should know.


III. Build and flash
====================

  $ source poky/oe-init-build-env build
  $ bitbake-layers add-layer ../meta-rzg3l-fast-logo-intro
  $ bitbake core-image-weston

The frames are installed into /boot of the rootfs, partition 2 of the wic
image. U-Boot itself is in bl2/fip, and how that reaches the board depends on
the boot mode (RZ/G3L Linux Start-up Guide):

  QSPI boot (the default, SW_MODE OFF OFF OFF ON)
      Write bl2_bp_spi-smarc-rzg3l.srec and fip-smarc-rzg3l.srec with Flash
      Writer (Start-up Guide 4.3-4.5). Writing the wic does NOT update U-Boot.
  eSD boot (SW_MODE ON ON OFF ON)
      The wic image carries bl2_bp_esd and fip at its start, so writing the
      wic to the card updates U-Boot too (Start-up Guide 8.3).
  eMMC boot
      Start-up Guide 8.2.

Do this again after any change to the U-Boot side, including a new resolution
or frame pace.

A board flashed with the new wic but an old or stock fip boots normally: U-Boot
shows nothing, and the kernel, which is not told otherwise, initialises the
display itself.


IV. Configuration
=================

All of these go in local.conf.

Frames
------

  RZG3L_FASTLOGO_FRAMES = "l1.bmp l2.bmp l3.bmp l4.bmp"      (default)

The frames U-Boot plays, in order. Each entry is a file name looked up in
FILESPATH (this layer's recipes-graphics/rzg3l-fastlogo-frames/files, or a
bbappend's), or an absolute path on the build host:

  RZG3L_FASTLOGO_FRAMES = "/home/me/logo/a.bmp /home/me/logo/b.bmp"

The number of frames is the number listed; U-Boot plays whatever is installed.
Frames must be uncompressed 24 or 32 bpp BMP, drawn at exactly
RZG3L_FASTLOGO_RESOLUTION. The build checks this. At 1920x1080 up to 14 frames
fit in the reserved memory, at 1280x720 up to 31.

  RZG3L_FASTLOGO_PACKAGE = ""

builds an image without the frames. U-Boot then scans out a black frame and
the hand-over still takes place.

Frame pace
----------

  RZG3L_FASTLOGO_RATE_MS = "1000"                              (default)

The time each frame is held, compiled into U-Boot. It is rounded down to whole
output frames (16.7 ms at 60 Hz).

Resolution
----------

  RZG3L_FASTLOGO_RESOLUTION = "1920x1080"                      (default)
  RZG3L_FASTLOGO_RESOLUTION = "1280x720"

The mode U-Boot drives. The kernel needs nothing: U-Boot tells it the mode at
boot. Change the artwork with it.

The U-Boot driver does not read EDID; every resolution is a table of timings
compiled in. To add one, in the U-Boot driver (a new patch after 0010, or edit
0008 in place):

  1. Copy drivers/video/rzg2l/define_1080p.h to a new header and fill in
     PLL7, D-PHY, DSI link, DU and ADV7535 timings for the mode, and its
     LCD_CEA_VIC. The VIC must name a CEA-861 mode with the same timing - the
     kernel pins its HDMI connector to that mode.
  2. In drivers/video/rzg2l/rzg2l_video.c add one line pair to the table:

       #elif (RZG2L_LOGO_WIDTH == 1024) && (RZG2L_LOGO_HEIGHT == 768)
       #include "define_1024x768.h"

The ADV7535 tops out at a 148.5 MHz pixel clock, i.e. 1920x1080@60. The build
names the file to edit if RZG3L_FASTLOGO_RESOLUTION has no table.

Without Yocto, the same two macros select the table on the compiler command
line (make KCFLAGS="-DRZG2L_LOGO_WIDTH=1280 -DRZG2L_LOGO_HEIGHT=720"), and
RZG2L_LOGO_RATE_MS sets the pace. Built without them the driver uses 1080p and
1000 ms.

U-Boot environment
------------------

For experiments on the board, without a rebuild (setenv, then saveenv):

  logo_rate_ms     frame time in ms, overrides RZG3L_FASTLOGO_RATE_MS
  logo_frames      play at most this many frames, even if more are installed
  logo_mmc_dev     0 (eMMC) or 1 (microSD); by default the medium is detected
                   the way the BSP's bootcmd_check does it
  logo_handover    "no" leaves the display to the kernel, which then brings
                   it up the stock way - useful for comparison

All four are read at power-on, before bootcmd runs, so saved values take
effect even though the BSP's default bootcmd starts with "env default -a".


V. How the hand-over is declared
================================

The kernel drivers inherit the pipeline only when their device tree nodes
carry "renesas,seamless-boot-logo". This layer does not put that in the board
.dts. U-Boot adds it at booti, from ft_system_setup() (CONFIG_OF_SYSTEM_SETUP),
and only when it has actually started the pipeline:

  /soc/vsp@10870000            renesas,seamless-boot-logo
  /soc/display@10890000        renesas,seamless-boot-logo
  /soc/dsi@10850000            renesas,seamless-boot-logo
  /soc/i2c@10090800/hdmi@3d    renesas,seamless-boot-logo
                               renesas,seamless-boot-logo-vic = <VIC>
                               interrupts-extended deleted

So the same kernel boots correctly under any bootloader: with this one it
inherits the running display, with any other it initialises the display as
usual. If a node is missing, or a write fails, nothing is declared - and every
such case prints a "fast-logo:" line saying why.

Why not the EVT_FT_FIXUP event: on this board OFNODE_MULTI_TREE is off, and
then the event is only raised for U-Boot's own control tree, never for the
kernel's. Why not ft_board_setup(): board/renesas/rcar-common already defines
it, and the BSP keeps OF_BOARD_SETUP off for RZ/G3L on purpose.


VI. Checking that it worked
===========================

U-Boot console:

  fast-logo: 1000 ms per frame
  fast-logo: intro from mmc 1:2
  fast-logo: intro is 4 frame(s)
  fast-logo: intro armed, <n> headers, parks on frame 3
  ...
  fast-logo: hand-over declared to the kernel, CEA VIC 16
  Starting kernel ...

In Linux:

  # ls /proc/device-tree/soc/i2c@10090800/hdmi@3d/ | grep seamless
  renesas,seamless-boot-logo
  renesas,seamless-boot-logo-vic
  # ls /boot/fastlogo_*
  # cat /sys/class/drm/card0-HDMI-A-1/modes
  1920x1080

In the build:

  $ strings tmp/deploy/images/smarc-rzg3l/fip.bin | grep fastlogo_
  boot/fastlogo_%ux%u_%02u.bin.gz


VII. Notes for applications
===========================

  - The HDMI connector reports exactly one mode, the one U-Boot drives, and
    always reports connected (see VIII). Use it; asking for another mode fails.
  - The first commit takes the display over, and its content goes on screen
    at once. Anything committed after that behaves as on a stock kernel.
    Whatever commits first wins, so nothing may commit before the program
    that is meant to own the display.
  - Opening the DRM device makes a program DRM master only if no one else is.
    If the compositor is also going to start, order the application before it
    (Before=weston.service) and release the device - or DRM master - before
    the compositor needs it.
  - There is no /dev/fb0 (section VIII): draw through DRM/KMS, as the
    sample application does.


VIII. Known limitations
=======================

  - The HDMI connector is pinned to the one mode U-Boot programmed for the
    lifetime of the system: it always reports connected, EDID is not read and
    hot-plug is not reported. A sink that cannot display the mode gets no
    picture rather than a fallback.
  - No in-kernel client may commit before userspace, so fastlogo.cfg turns
    off the framebuffer console and the kernel logo (the console commits a
    black frame at probe), DRM fbdev emulation (it commits its blank
    framebuffer from the hotplug event that follows its own first connector
    probe, a few seconds into boot) and the console on the virtual terminal
    (CONFIG_VT_CONSOLE, which needs CONFIG_EXPERT; this also removes the VT
    switch on suspend). Consequences: no /dev/fb0, so applications drawing
    through fbdev do not work; no kernel messages on HDMI. The serial console
    is unaffected. The build fails if any of these is back on in the final
    kernel .config.
  - The "splash" image feature is removed (conf/layer.conf): a boot splash
    would draw over the logo.
  - 0x58000000..0x5fffffff (128 MiB) is reserved for the lifetime of the
    system, and the MIPI DSI and LCDC clocks are never gated.
  - The BSP's U-Boot environment has loadaddr=0x58000000, which is frame 00.
    A manual "ext4load mmc 0:2 - <file>" or "tftp <file>" at the U-Boot prompt
    overwrites the picture; pass an explicit address.
  - The frames are loaded from partition 2 (ext4) of the MMC device the board
    boots from. Other boot media show a black frame.
  - The hand-over is declared by the device tree fix-up of bootm/booti. A
    boot path that does not run it (bootefi is untested) simply does not
    declare it, and the kernel initialises the display the stock way.
  - The artwork shipped here is the RZ/G3E sample content. Confirm its
    distribution terms before publishing an image or this layer.
